I had just posted about the death of my best wizard a couple of days
ago, when I had lost my interest in NetHack. Well, I decided to try
again, and had excellent luck with my new neutral wizard, doing quite
well and this time actually getting poison resistance! I started out
with the sleep spell, which aided greatly up to the end of the game, as
even up to then, my only offensive spell was force bolt.
    There were shops on levels 3, 4, 5, and 6, where I obtained my
excellent and extensive food supply, my speed boots, and my helm of
brilliance.
    I was able to find a blessed magic lamp in minetown, and after a
few tries, I managed to procure a djinni, who gave me a blessed +0
SDSM, which was quite fine for me, bringing my AC down to -4. Then I
decided to bless my potion of water at the temple, but forgot to drop
it before praying. Oh, well... I moved aside a square and read an
unid'd scroll, which burnt up my leather gloves (eventually dropped)
and pissed off the priestess.
   No matter! I thought, and just moved outside, and dug portals into
the temple from outside, and began to shoot the priestess with force
bolts, to no avail. She bogged me down in insects, but thanks to my
excellent armour I took minimal damage, despite my strangely low HP,
which was somehow around 50 or so despite my being level 9, I think.
However, my mana was notably high, in the hundreds already, so after
raising a level off of the bugs, I took a break, and the next day came
back, exploring the rest of the mines. At the lowest level I went to ,
there was a darkened maze-thing, in which I found 3 spellbooks before I
left.
     The first was "charm monster", a spell which I had never had
before, but had heard good things about. I was able to cast it with a
0% fail. The next was "create monster", which I used a few times later
on, but it wasn't too important. Lastly came the all-powerful
"identify", which had a 77% fail, but I was able to lower this to about
50% with another level or two, and then to 0% with an enhancement to
divination spells.
     I returned to Minetown after practicing charm monster, getting
some interesting though worthless pets that I didn't bring with me.
There I went back to the temple, and was hit twice by summon insects,
and I charmed both rounds of the spell. I moved aside to recharge my
mana, and returned to the temple again, and rushed up to the priestess.
She took me down to about 20 hp in one hit, which frightened me, but I
still cast charm monster with 17 mana left, and was able to restore
peace in Minetown, and finally procured my holy water.
     This I used on a stack of scrolls that I had just blanked at the
Oracle*, and this became the first time in which I had both some blank
scrolls AND a magic marker on which to use them. So now I had a blessed
+4 quarterstaff, a blessed +5 cloak of magic resistance, a blessed +3
helm of brilliance (giving me 20 int and wis), an uncursed +0 pair of
speed boots, and a burnt uncursed pair of leather gloves. I ditched the
gloves and moved on.
     After several dramatic battles in which charm monster became the
principal player, spending 10k on protection (got 5), getting my hp up
to 100 by dancing with a couple of nurses, encountering 2 more altars
(1 aligned and one chaotic), another temple, and finally procuring an
uncursed set of gloves, I went further down and found a cloudy potion
which I for some reason didn't bother to identify... oh dear.
     "You merge with your set of silver dragon scales! You rip out of
your cloak! You drop your weapon! You drop the rest of your armour! Use
#monster to use your ice breath and #sit to lay eggs."
     Oh well, I thought, it could have been worse -- I still had
reflection, I could now use cold bolts at will, I could create an army
of dragons, and my other items were intact. Great! I laid as many eggs
as my pack could hold (about 6 or 7 more, as I didn't have a BoH) and
went to the next level. After a room or two, I stumbled onto a
disgusting nest! Ok, so I hightailed it out of there, letting my babies
do the work... but all they managed to do was get into my way. A
cockatrice had followed me to the room I had retreated to, so I said
what the heck, and, not wanting to blast my baby silver dragons, walked
into it.
     "The cockatrice bites! You turn to stone."
     Ok, so dragons petrify even faster than humans?

